"Please build a strategy deck storyline for the following brief. Audience: Meridian Therapeutics board of directors. Decision: whether to accelerate Phase III enrollment for compound MX-7 or pause pending interim safety data.\n\n---\n**BRIEF: MX-7 Program Update — Board Review, Q4 2026**\n\nClinical progress: MX-7 completed Phase IIb with 68% response rate vs. 31% placebo (p<0.001), n=142. Enrollment pace in Phase III is currently 14 sites active out of 22 contracted, adding ~40 patients/month. At this rate, Phase III completes enrollment in 22 months.\n\nIf we activate the remaining 8 sites now, enrollment pace rises to ~72 patients/month and completion falls to 12 months — a 10-month acceleration worth an estimated $180M in NPV (internal model, 12% discount rate).\n\nSafety: Phase IIb showed a 4.2% incidence of Grade 3 liver enzyme elevation, all reversible. The interim DSMB review is scheduled in 6 weeks. Protocol allows acceleration before DSMB review; it does not require waiting.\n\nCompetitive context: Vantara Bio filed an IND for a competing compound in September. If MX-7 reaches market 10 months earlier, our internal model projects 34% higher peak market share due to first-mover pricing.\n\nFinancial: Activating the remaining 8 sites costs $22M upfront. Full program budget is $310M; $118M spent to date.\n\nFootnote 1: The $180M NPV figure assumes FDA standard review (12 months post-filing). If the compound receives Priority Review designation — which our regulatory team rates as 60% probable — NPV increases to $290M.\n\nFootnote 2: The 34% market share premium is modeled on historical first-mover data from oncology indications. MX-7 targets a rare metabolic disorder where first-mover data is sparse; the regulatory team flags this estimate as speculative.\n\n---\nPlease produce the full storyline: governing thought, argument tree, action-title slide list, per-slide specs, and self-check."